Borehole Gossamer

Ingredients: OLD SKOOL JUNGLE & DRUM 'N' BASS + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Trumpet + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Invented two kilometers underground in 1974 by Yorkshire coal miners drumming frantically on high-pressure zinc ventilation ducts to stay sane, inadvertently creating lightning-fast polyrhythms cushioned by warm subterranean air pockets.

Borehole Gossamer sounds like a frantic, 168-BPM warehouse rave detonating inside an infinite, mist-filled cathedral carved of coal. Blistering, syncopated breaks hammered out on resonant industrial ducting collide with soaring, Harmon-muted 1970s jazz-fusion trumpet lines that stretch through cavernous tape delays, wrapped in weightless, diaphanous vocal sighs that make claustrophobic depths feel like open sky.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 162–174 BPM; frantic, unquantized Old Skool Jungle breakbeats hammered out on ringing metallic surfaces, counterbalanced by a languid, floating half-time jazz sway.
- Harmonic Palette: Modal jazz suspensions straight out of 1970s CTI-records catalogs (Fm9, Dbm11, Bmaj7#11), shifting unexpectedly into airy, weightless whole-tone scales and open-fifth ambient drones.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Duct-Cavity Reverb: routing percussion and brass through literal 50-meter coiled zinc ventilation tubing miked at opposing ends.
  2. Varispeed Flutter-Flanging: feeding 1970s open-reel B-77 tape decks through manually wobbled capstans to give soaring trumpets a shimmering, airborne instability.
  3. Methane-Gate Compression: extreme, hyper-pumping sidechain compression keyed to cavernous sub-bass hits, creating an airy, gasping suction effect between rapid-fire percussion bursts.
- Dynamics: Songs inhale in vast, suspended silences—filled only with distant trumpet wails and warm hiss—before suddenly collapsing into blinding avalanches of hyper-speed duct-percussion, breathing between crushing velocity and floating weightlessness.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born during the rolling British power outages of the mid-1970s, the genre emerged two kilometers beneath the North Sea in the sweltering, dark haulage ways of Ellington Colliery. Trapped during a shaft compressor stall, a shift of miners kept their spirits alive by banging copper wrenches against pressurized exhaust flues in relentless, syncopated gallops, while colliery brass band cornetist Donald Vance improvised modal runs into the suction intake, discovering the acoustic feedback created an angelic, swirling halo of sound. Soon, an underground youth subculture formed: the "Vane-Tappers." Dressed in grease-splattered indigo denim boilersuits tailored with extravagant 1970s velvet lapels, brass-trimmed pit helmets, and northern-soul dancing loafers, they would hijack abandoned air-return drifts on weekends. Hooking pirate battery packs to tape-echo units and brass horns, they blew ecstatic, high-speed underground jazz-raves in absolute darkness, using miner headlamps as strobe lights.

Typical Instruments
- Lead Voice: 1970s Martin Committee Trumpet (equipped with vintage Harmon mutes, run through Maestro Echoplex tape delays and Mu-Tron III envelope filters).
- Core Percussion: Galvanized zinc ventilation pipes, steel manifold housings, and copper pit spanners played with rattan sticks to emulate chopped Amen breaks.
- Synthesizers & Keys: Mellotron M400 (running degraded 8-Choir and Brass tape frames), Fender Rhodes Mark I run through vintage Roland Space Echoes.
- Low End: Overwound acoustic upright bass and modified 1970s Minimoog sub-oscillators routed through subterranean tunnel PA horns.

For Fans Of: LTJ Bukem, Miles Davis (late-1970s Pangaea era), Cocteau Twins, early Goldie.

